<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakshmana Sena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1178–<a href="/years/1206">1206</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakshmana Sena was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-dynasty">Sena dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> who reigned from about 1178 to about 1206. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature, he kept at his court the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a>. Around 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a> surprised him at Nadia, and he fled to eastern Bengal, where his dynasty survived for some decades while the west fell to Muslim rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lakshmana Sena was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballal-sena">Ballala Sena</a>, whom he succeeded. In his youth he led campaigns against Gauda, Kamarupa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, then ruled by the Gahadavala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a>, helping his grandfather Vijaya Sena and his father to extend the borders of the Sena kingdom. He came to the throne himself at an advanced age. His reign is known from the inscriptions of his time, among them the Deopara Prashasti, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of his successors and from the Tabaqat-i-Nasiri, written by 1260.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At its height his kingdom held the territories of Gauda, Kamrupa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and Kashi. After subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in 1200, the Turko-Afghan invader Bakhtiyar entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> with a band of trained horsemen, received at first in Nadia as a horse dealer. The king, then over eighty and at his dinner, was taken by surprise. Bakhtiyar went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a>, while Lakshmana Sena's sons kept Sena rule alive in the south-east for some time. A laudatory verse of Sarana, by contrast, praises a victory of the king over a Mlechchha ruler, perhaps a Muslim one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The king composed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poems himself and completed the Adbhuta Sagara, a work his father had left unfinished. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a> his court poets included Dhoyin and Sharan, and the courtiers Shridharadasa, Halayudh Mishra and Umapati Dhara wrote treatises and literary works. He founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> and established a calendar, the Lakshmana Era, that remained in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> for at least 400 years. A devoted Vaishnava, he took titles such as Paramavaisnava. His piety and generosity impressed even the chronicler Minhaj-i-Siraj, who called him a great Rai of Bengal and compared him with Sultan Qutubuddin Aibak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lakshmana Sena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji, a Turkic-Afghan commander serving the Ghurids, raided Bihar and then, around 1204, surprised the Sena king <b>Lakshmana Sena</b> at Nadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/khalji-dynasty-of-bengal">Khalji dynasty of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
